---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { Code } from "astro:components";

title: "Contributing";
description: "How to contribute to the Umi‑Time project.";
---

<Base title="Contributing">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <main class="page page-contributing">
      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Contributing</h1>
        <p>
          Umi-Time gives us one shared number for the exact same instant globally, while presenting it in a way that is locally meaningful. 
          As an open standard ecosystem, contributions are welcome across our technical specifications, website interfaces, and reference libraries.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Project Architecture -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Project Architecture</h2>
        <p>The project is modularized across independent, specialized repositories:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="https://github.com">umi-time/spec</a></strong> — The canonical, self-contained standard document defining the 0ᵘ–360ᵘ mapping rules, mathematical formulas, and vendored MathJax layouts.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="https://github.com">umi-time/www</a></strong> — The primary public website, the Manifesto, and the presentation layers (Clock, Map, Geometry). It embeds the <code>spec</code> repository dynamically via a Git submodule.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong><a href="https://github.com">umi-time/umi-time-js</a></strong> — The reference JavaScript/TypeScript implementation library for core parsing, calculation, and rendering.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Contributing to the Specification -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Contributing to the Specification Standard</h2>
        <p>
          Because the <code>spec</code> repository maintains the immutable standard, changes to formulas or core architectures follow a strict protocol. 
          Typos, formatting bugs, or ambiguities can be submitted directly via an Issue in the <code>spec</code> repository. Architectural adjustments require community consensus on a Discussion thread prior to drafting a Pull Request.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Getting Started -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Getting Started with the Website</h2>
        <p>To contribute to the website layout, documentation text, or interactive tools, clone the web repository locally:</p>

        <Code
          code={`git clone https://github.com.git
cd www
git submodule update --init --recursive
npm install`}
          lang="bash"
          theme="dracula"
        />

        <p>
          <strong>Important First-Time Setup Note:</strong> Our <code>astro.config.mjs</code> features an automated asset sync script that mirrors submodule layouts and MathJax layouts into your active <code>public/</code> tree. **You must run the compilation sequence at least once** to populate these asset structures properly before initializing your dev server:
        </p>

        <Code
          code={`npm run build`}
          lang="bash"
          theme="dracula"
        />

        <p>Once compiled, start Astro's local hot-reloading development loop:</p>

        <Code
          code={`npm run dev`}
          lang="bash"
          theme="dracula"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Website Contributions -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Website Contributions</h2>
        <p>
          The primary public interface lives in the <code>www</code> repository and is powered by Astro. 
          Impactful contributions include:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>Improving documentation copy and text layout readability.</li>
          <li>Resolving responsive design framework or styling bugs across viewports.</li>
          <li>Enhancing the interactive canvas demonstration layers (the clock dial, world map tracking, or solar visualizations).</li>
          <li>Drafting new implementation examples, setup recipes, or technical tutorials.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Making Changes -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Making Changes</h2>
        <p>Before opening a pull request on any repository:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Ensure TypeScript builds cleanly without errors</li>
          <li>Run the repository test suite if applicable</li>
          <li>Follow existing code style and naming conventions</li>
          <li>Update documentation if your change affects a public API or interface behavior</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Coding & Interface Standards -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Coding & Documentation Standards</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Universal First:</strong> Keep calculations and core records mapped strictly to UTC. Never write regional adjustments into underlying data objects.</li>
          <li><strong>Presentation Agnostic:</strong> Interface logic should treat local meaning as a presentation mapping layer—supporting visual, textual, and digital formats equally without favoring a singular UI layout.</li>
          <li><strong>Zero JS Footprint by Default:</strong> The specification text page parses static raw HTML elements server-side via Astro's <code>set:html</code> directive. Keep script inclusions tightly scoped.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Library Coding Standards -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Library Coding Standards</h2>
        <p>When contributing code to the <code>umi-time-js</code> core reference library, adhere to these architectural rules:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use TypeScript for all core library code.</li>
          <li>Prefer pure functions for solar geometry and time conversion calculations.</li>
          <li>Keep the <code>UmiTime</code> engine immutable—all transformation methods must return new instances.</li>
          <li>Document public methods explicitly with clear intent, parameter definitions, and geometric units.</li>
          <li>Write comprehensive tests for any new solar tracking or coordinate mapping functions.</li>
          <li>Ensure all formatting spec constants remain strictly synchronized with the canonical Umi‑Time standard.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Pull Requests -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Pull Requests</h2>
        <p>Every pull request should:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Build successfully inside its independent repository pipeline</li>
          <li>Pass all validation checks and tests</li>
          <li>Include updated documentation if needed</li>
          <li>Reference related issues or discussions</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Keep PRs focused. Small, well-scoped changes are significantly easier to review and merge.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Issues & Feature Requests -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Issues & Feature Requests</h2>
        <p>If you find a bug or want to propose a feature, open an issue on the specific repository matching the component.</p>

        <p>Please include:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Clear reproduction steps</li>
          <li>Expected vs actual behavior</li>
          <li>Environment details (browser, OS, Node version)</li>
          <li>Any relevant Umi‑Time input values or coordinates</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Thanks -->
      <section class="example">
        <h2>Thank You</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time is a voluntary, collaborative effort to refine how a connected species communicates about time. Whether you’re correcting an equation typo, translating document layouts, or constructing a client library, your help is deeply appreciated.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
